{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Ground Speed at a Constant Heading.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Ground Speed at a Constant Heading.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ground speed at a constant heading ground speed at a constant heading","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Ground Speed at a Constant Heading.md","fileName":"Ground Speed at a Constant Heading.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Ground Speed at a Constant Heading","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 21:32\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Wind Navigation and Flight Range]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Wind Navigation and Flight Range","Ground Speed on a Desired Track","flightscience.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Ground Speed at a Constant Heading.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Ground Speed at a Constant Heading.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ground speed at a constant heading ground speed at a constant heading","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Ground Speed at a Constant Heading.md","fileName":"Ground Speed at a Constant Heading.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Ground Speed at a Constant Heading","heading":"Ground Speed at a Constant Heading","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"When aircraft heading is held fixed, wind changes both the magnitude and direction of the ground-velocity vector. A perpendicular wind adds a sideways component and can make the magnitude of ground velocity slightly greater than true airspeed, even though the resulting track drifts. This differs from [[Ground Speed on a Desired Track]], where heading is allowed to change to preserve course.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Wind Navigation and Flight Range","Ground Speed on a Desired Track","flightscience.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Gr